      "math_academy_id": "q-216317",
      "sequence_position": 12,
      "problem": "Let $x\\in R.$ Prove that if $|x|<0,$ then $x\\in Q.$\n\nThe theorem can be written as the implication \n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\\,⇒\\,{{field-2}}.\n$$\n\nThe {{field-3}}$\\,${{field-4}} of our implication is always {{field-5}}.\n\nTherefore, the theorem is \n{{field-6}}$\\,${{field-7}}.",
      "worked_solution": "We say that an implication has a *vacuous proof* if the antecedent is always false: \n\n$$\n\\overset{\\text{Always false}}{\\overset{⏞}{P}}⇒\\,Q\n$$\n\nIt does not matter whether the consequent is true or false. \n\nWith that in mind, we proceed as follows:\n\nThe theorem can be written as the implication \n\n$$\n|x|<0\\,⇒\\,x\\in Q.\n$$\n\nNow, since $|x|$ is non-negative for all $x\\in R,$ we have the following fact:\n\nThe antecedent $|x|<0$ of our implication is always false.\n\nFinally, we can make the following conclusion:\n\nTherefore, the theorem is vacuously true.",
